[Music]  our series we call it a more perfect  union aims to show that what unites us  as Americans is far greater than what  divides us this morning we're  introducing you to a grocery store owner  in a remote Alaska community he went the  extra mile to make sure his customers  got the food and supplies they needed  when the coronavirus well left them  stranded CBS this morning Saturday  Cohoes as Dana Jacobson shows us his  remarkable story in southeastern Alaska  the town of Gustavus prides itself on  its closeness to nature  well we're a small community our economy  pretty much revolves around the tourist  industry  we're the Gateway to Glacier Bay  National Park there are no roads to  Gustavus it's only accessible by charter  plane or ship as Alaskans you know we're  pretty resourceful and we're kind of  used to this kind of thing we're cut off  by Nature up here  Alaska's state-run ferry is a critical  service providing transport and  delivering supplies to this remote  community but when a short shut down for  repairs was extended because of kovat 19  the town was left without options for  months businesses like Kelley  McLaughlin's gallery and coffee shop  were struggling things like thousand  pounds of coffee beans and orders of  milk there's no way I would be able to  afford shipping everything up over on  sea planes Joshua Parker or Tosh as he's  known by the locals thank you  runs Gustavus is only grocery store and  had the only ship in town able to make  supply runs just on a gamble the gamble  was you know what happens if the ferry  quit we would be totally cut off so  let's make this investment in this boat  kind of his insurance policy  that gamble paid off keeping the town of  450 residents supplied with food by  making weekly voyages to the state  capital of Juneau the 60 mile trek takes  14 hours by sea a little more than what  people are used to from their local  grocer and you know I I wish we could  just have a truck pull up to the back of  our store and open up and there's all  the groceries for the week that'd be  really slick but it's just you know in  rural Alaska it's not the way it is it  feels like you're a lifeline for the  people there right now these are all  neighbors these are friends they're  people you care about we're gonna do  whatever it takes and it's not just  groceries virtually everything coming in  to Gustavus arrives aboard Tasha's ship  we're bringing all kinds of stuff for  people be it lumber I mean if you need a  new fridge we'll try to find a fridge  and get it brought in here so whatever  people need yeah I mean everything from  building supplies to food everything  comes in roll-on/roll-off that ferry and  and he had to replace that somehow what  has that meant to your town to have him  taking care of everyone in that sort of  way if we didn't have any way to get our  groceries and supplies and whatever here  a lot of people would have to move away  go somewhere else is this hitting you  financially well sure I mean there's no  question that running the boat is  exponentially more expensive than using  the ferry the gal that does our books  has a conniption everyday you know it's  not a good time to be raising prices of  groceries that's for sure so we've been  holding the prices just eaten the  difference to get that boat keep the  prices the same it was huge I wouldn't  have been able to operate look out for  your neighbor that's kind of the  attitude we have here we care about each  other we take care of each other and  right now more than ever that's probably  a message I think people need so that's  what I would say with that  for CBS this morning I'm Dana Jacobson  look out for your neighbor you go Tosh  we're happy to say that the Gustavus  ferry is now back up it's running people  in town told us they are grateful to  Tosh for helping them for more than six  months while the ferry was shut down  you know guys frito-lay that makes those  wonderful barbecue lay potato chips they  have a campaign that says joy givers and  so when I think of joy givers you think  of people like Tosh he's got a nice face  kind heart really nice guy a joy giver  yes true Gail although I will I will say  I prefer the plain Lay's potato chips to  the barbecue

